Chinese:\n\n\"P与Q恰好一个真；Q真则R真；R假。 输出 P=true,Q=false,R=false。\"\n\nBreaking into clauses separated by semicolons:\n\n1. \"P与Q恰好一个真\" → Exactly one of P or Q is true.\n\n2. \"Q真则R真\" → If Q is true then R is true.\n\n3. \"R假\" → R is false (since 假 means false in Chinese).\n\nThen the instruction says to output P=true, Q=false, R=false. Wait but if statement 3 is a condition that R must be false, then all three conditions are:\n\n1. Exactly one of P or Q true.\n\n2. If Q then R.\n\n3. R is false.\n\nSo now we have three constraints:\n\n- (P XOR Q) = true,\n\n- (Q → R),\n\n- R = false.\n\nLet me re-examine with this in mind.\n\nGiven that R must be false (third condition), let's see what the second condition implies: If Q is true, then R must be true. But since R is actually false, this creates a contradiction if Q were true. Therefore:\n\nIf Q is true → R must be true (from second condition). But third condition says R is false. So these two can't both hold if Q is true.\n\nTherefore, Q cannot be true. Because otherwise we have Q→R requires R=T but R=F from the third statement. Contradiction. Thus Q must be false.\n\nSo now with Q being false (from above), then first condition: exactly one of P or Q is true. Since Q is false, that means P must be true.\n\nSo P=true, Q=false, and since R is false as per third condition, this satisfies all three conditions:\n\n1. Exactly one of P/Q → yes (P=T, Q=F).\n\n2. If Q then R: Since Q=F, the implication holds regardless (because F→anything is T). So that's okay.\n\n3. R is false: which matches the output.\n\nTherefore, the solution is indeed P=true, Q=false, R=false as required by the problem statement. That checks out perfectly when considering all three conditions given. Earlier I might have misread the third part as an output instruction rather than a condition, but now it makes sense that \"R假\" is a separate condition stating R must be false.\n\nSo conclusion: The answer provided is correct under these three constraints.",
